WEBVTT

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:15.050
Well, I'm not convinced that the work is as
urgent as this report suggests, so perhaps

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:16.050
we should&hellip;

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.559
Oh come on, the building is practically falling
down!

00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:22.889
Sorry Lin, I don't think Tan had finished.
We'll get to you in a minute.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.029
Sorry.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:29.909
As I was saying, perhaps we should get a second
opinion before we spend any money.

00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:33.729
Thank you Tan. What's your opinion Walter?

00:00:33.729 --> 00:00:38.189
Well, as far as I'm concerned, it's a question
of safety. So I think we should go ahead.

00:00:38.189 --> 00:00:40.518
Are you suggesting that someone could get
hurt?

00:00:40.518 --> 00:00:45.729
In my opinion, yes. If you ask me, there is
a serious risk of an accident. And it's not

00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:49.539
a recent problem.
Are you implying we should have done something

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:50.539
earlier?

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:52.878
Much earlier. It's a real concern.

00:00:52.878 --> 00:00:58.489
Hear hear!
In that case, I agree. We should do something

00:00:58.489 --> 00:00:59.489
now.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.709
I think so too.
Thank you Walter. Well, if there's no,

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.049
Excuse me Madame Chairperson.

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:07.049
Yes Barbara.

00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:11.449
What about the problem with parking? There
were no places again this morning.

00:01:11.449 --> 00:01:13.459
Maybe if you got to work on time.

00:01:13.459 --> 00:01:18.158
All comments through the chair if you don't
mind Walter. Parking isn't on the agenda for

00:01:18.158 --> 00:01:24.079
this meeting. Perhaps you could suggest it
for our next meeting Barbara. Well, if there's

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:26.099
no more discussion, we'll put it to a vote.

00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:38.539
Hear hear!
Today we're looking again at meetings. In

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:43.968
meetings, especially formal meetings, it's
important to keep the discussion relevant,

00:01:43.968 --> 00:01:49.379
and to the point. So we're looking at some
words and phrases that a Chairperson might

00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:57.178
use to stop irrelevant discussion and interruptions.
We're also looking at ways of stating an opinion,

00:01:57.178 --> 00:02:00.608
and agreeing or disagreeing.

00:02:00.608 --> 00:02:05.659
First of all, notice how Tan states that he
doesn't agree with the report.

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.439
Well, I'm not convinced that the work is as
urgent as this report suggests.

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:17.439
Tan says, "I'm not convinced." He means he
disagrees with the report. But by saying he's

00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:23.489
not convinced - he is leaving himself open
to another point of view.

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:28.279
Let's look at some phrases you can use to
express disagreement.

00:02:28.279 --> 00:02:33.289
I'm afraid I'm not convinced.

00:02:33.289 --> 00:02:37.769
I don't entirely agree.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:41.999
I'm not sure about that.

00:02:41.999 --> 00:02:46.429
I can't agree with you there.

00:02:46.429 --> 00:02:53.709
I don't think that's quite correct.
Notice that all of these ways of disagreeing

00:02:53.709 --> 00:03:01.299
are polite. If you use stronger terms of disagreement,
it can sound rude, or argumentative.

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:05.830
Well, I'm not convinced that the work is as
urgent as this report suggests, so perhaps.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.139
Don't be ridiculous!

00:03:08.139 --> 00:03:12.469
Excuse me?
When disagreeing, the speaker normally also

00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:18.109
gives a reason for their opinion, and may
use an expression showing that it is their

00:03:18.109 --> 00:03:23.089
opinion. Listen to Walter.
Well, as far as I'm concerned, it's a question

00:03:23.089 --> 00:03:25.829
of safety. So I think we should go ahead.

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:28.179
Are you suggesting that someone could get
hurt?

00:03:28.179 --> 00:03:32.109
In my opinion, es.
Let's practise some phrases you can use to

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:38.539
introduce your opinion about something.
In my opinion

00:03:38.539 --> 00:03:39.900
As far as I'm concerned

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.329
If you ask me

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:53.419
The way I see it is this.
And of course you can simply state, "I think"

00:03:53.419 --> 00:03:56.289
or "I believe".

00:03:56.289 --> 00:04:01.929
Notice that Tan asks for clarification of
the point Walter makes. He wants to make sure

00:04:01.929 --> 00:04:05.529
he understands what Walter is saying.

00:04:05.529 --> 00:04:11.809
Let's look at some ways of seeking clarification,
and checking understanding. Practise these

00:04:11.809 --> 00:04:15.369
with Tan.
Are you suggesting that someone could get

00:04:15.369 --> 00:04:18.959
hurt?

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.119
Are you saying that someone could get hurt?

00:04:24.119 --> 00:04:28.829
Do you mean someone could get hurt?

00:04:28.829 --> 00:04:35.959
Are you implying that someone could get hurt?
One of the roles of the Chairperson is to

00:04:35.959 --> 00:04:41.780
make sure everyone gets to have their say.
All discussion should go through the chair

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:48.199
- that is, people talk to the Chairperson,
not to each other directly. So the Chairperson

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:55.969
sometimes has to interrupt, or stop someone
from speaking. Let's see how Denise does this.

00:04:55.969 --> 00:05:01.269
Well, I'm not convinced that the work is as
urgent as this report suggests, so perhaps.

00:05:01.269 --> 00:05:04.749
Oh come on, the building is practically falling
down!

00:05:04.749 --> 00:05:09.079
Sorry Lin, I don't think Tan had finished.
We'll get to you in a minute.

00:05:09.079 --> 00:05:13.209
Sorry.
By saying, "I don't think Tan had finished",

00:05:13.209 --> 00:05:20.189
Denise is politely telling Lin that she shouldn't
interrupt, and Lin apologises. But what should

00:05:20.189 --> 00:05:23.719
you do if someone keeps trying to interrupt?
So perhaps,

00:05:23.719 --> 00:05:26.959
Oh come on - the building is practically falling
down!

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:30.889
Sorry Lin, I don't think Tan had finished.
We'll get to you in a minute.

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:32.699
Well it's obvious.

00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:35.409
Yes, but we do need to hear everyone's views
on this.

00:05:35.409 --> 00:05:36.829
Let's just vote on it.

00:05:36.829 --> 00:05:44.379
All in good time Lin. Please go on Tan.
Notice that the language the chairperson uses

00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:52.979
is always polite, but firm. She uses 'sorry'
'thank you' and 'please' as a way of respecting

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:57.979
the other members of the committee, although
she is in charge.

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:04.009
On the other hand, there is a time factor
- it may be necessary to cut people short.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:07.889
and furthermore, if we look more closely at
some of the estimates, we can see,

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:12.399
Excuse me - sorry Tan, perhaps we should move
on.

00:06:12.399 --> 00:06:13.579
I'm nearly finished.

00:06:13.579 --> 00:06:20.089
Yes but we do have to keep an eye on the time.
Perhaps we can come back to that point later.

00:06:20.089 --> 00:06:21.329
I think it's quite important.

00:06:21.329 --> 00:06:29.609
It is important, but I think it's more important
we hear everyone's views on this. Barbara?

00:06:29.609 --> 00:06:34.919
There were two key phrases used to stop Tan
from sending everyone to sleep. The first

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:41.759
one, "perhaps we should move on" is phrased
as a suggestion but it is said in a firm way,

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:44.469
with a falling intonation at the end.

00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:48.389
Practise with me:
Perhaps we should move on.

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:53.348
Perhaps we should move on.
This falling intonation makes it a more definite

00:06:53.348 --> 00:06:56.949
statement, rather than a suggestion.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:03.499
The second one was put as a reason for moving
on - that time is short. Nnotice the emphasis

00:07:03.499 --> 00:07:07.489
on the word 'do', and practise after me:
We do have to keep an eye on the time.

00:07:07.489 --> 00:07:14.669
We do have to keep an eye on the time.
Remember we said the discussion has to be

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:19.719
relevant. What do we do if someone makes an
irrelevant comment?

00:07:19.719 --> 00:07:24.009
What about the problem with parking. There
were no places again this morning.

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:25.999
Maybe if you got to work on time.

00:07:25.999 --> 00:07:30.709
All comments through the chair if you don't
mind Walter. Parking isn't on the agenda for

00:07:30.709 --> 00:07:36.119
this meeting. Perhaps you could suggest it
for our next meeting Barbara.

00:07:36.119 --> 00:07:40.098
When Walter makes a comment directly to Barbara,
Denise says:

00:07:40.098 --> 00:07:43.239
All comments through the Chair if you don't
mind.

00:07:43.239 --> 00:07:49.599
This is a way of reminding Walter of correct
meeting procedure. "All comments through the

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:56.089
Chair" means he must speak to the meeting,
not directly to one person at the table. Denise

00:07:56.089 --> 00:08:01.479
suggests that, "Parking isn't on the agenda
for this meeting". But so that Barbara doesn't

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:06.779
feel bad, she suggests it could be discussed
at another time.

00:08:06.779 --> 00:08:12.679
Finally, we've looked at disagreeing, Let's
look at ways of agreeing with a point.

00:08:12.679 --> 00:08:15.329
Are you implying we should have done something
earlier?

00:08:15.329 --> 00:08:17.848
Much earlier. It's a real concern.

00:08:17.848 --> 00:08:19.429
Hear hear!

00:08:19.429 --> 00:08:24.249
Well, in that case, I agree - we should do
something now.

00:08:24.249 --> 00:08:28.578
I think so too.
Agreeing is simpler than disagreeing, as you

00:08:28.578 --> 00:08:32.818
don't have to state a reason. Repeat these
phrases after me:

00:08:32.818 --> 00:08:34.349
I agree
I agree

00:08:34.349 --> 00:08:38.299
In that case, I agree.
In that case, I agree.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:41.619
I'll go along with you.
I'll go along with you.

00:08:41.619 --> 00:08:45.619
I couldn't agree more.
I couldn't agree more.

00:08:45.619 --> 00:08:48.429
That's right.
That's right.

00:08:48.429 --> 00:08:50.879
I concur.
I concur.

00:08:50.879 --> 00:08:55.828
Absolutely!
Absolutely!

00:08:55.828 --> 00:09:01.189
Let's review some of the other important expressions
we've looked at in today's episode.

00:09:01.189 --> 00:09:06.179
I'm afraid I'm not convinced.

00:09:06.179 --> 00:09:11.309
Are you suggesting that someone could get
hurt?

00:09:11.309 --> 00:09:15.319
I can't quite agree with you there.

00:09:15.319 --> 00:09:22.828
As far as I'm concerned, it's a question of
safety.

00:09:22.828 --> 00:09:26.889
Perhaps we should move on.

00:09:26.889 --> 00:09:35.189
We do have to keep an eye on the time.
When giving opinions in a meeting, it's important

00:09:35.189 --> 00:09:42.319
to use phrases such as 'in my opinion', 'from
my point of view' or 'I think', instead of

00:09:42.319 --> 00:09:47.599
just stating your opinions as facts. This
is respectful of other people who may have

00:09:47.599 --> 00:09:49.839
different views.

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:56.219
But when you strongly agree with something
someone else says, you can say 'Hear Hear!'

00:09:56.219 --> 00:09:59.739
That's all for today - see you next time on
The Business of English.